This project requires Flask and OpenCV for Python, I did research on how to use these using geeksforgeeks tutorials.

Flask

@app.route(/video)
def function()
  *code*
  return Response(*response*)
this will execute the function the moment you visit localhost/video, what is visible in the browser window is the object withing the parentheses of Response()


OpenCV

camera = cv2.VideoCapture(0)
this is the camera

.isOpened()
returns true or false depending on if the camera is opened or not

succes, frame = camera.read()
captures a frame with the camera, succes is a boolean that indicates if the framecapture has succeeded or not

succes, buffer = cv2.imencode('.jpg', frame)
reformats the frame to a .jpg


Python

frameBytes = buffer.tobytes
reformats buffer to raw bytes

yield(*waarde*)
is like a return, but doesn't terminate the function and returns a stream of data instead of individual data packets

mimetype = 'multipart/x-mixed-replace; boundary=frame'
mimetype is the kind of content the browser expects, multipart/x-mixed-replace is often used for videostreams, boundary=frame is used to differentiate frames in the data stream.

















